  "question": {
    "text": "Let \\(f:[1,3] \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}\\) be continuous and be derivable in \\((1,3)\\) and \\(f^{\\prime}(x)=[f(x)]^2+4 \\forall x \\in(1,3)\\). Then",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "mEFFh",
      "text": "\\(f(3)-f(1)=5\\) holds",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AByca",
      "text": "\\(f(3)-f(1)=5\\) does not hold",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "bGa6d",
      "text": "\\(f(3)-f(1)=3\\) holds",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "c2bV1",
      "text": "\\(f(3)-f(1)=4\\) holds",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "\\(\\begin{aligned}\n& \\text { Hint : } f^{\\prime}(c)=(f(c))^2+4=\\frac{f(3)-f(1)}{2} \\\\\n& \\Rightarrow f(3)-f(1)=2(f(c))^2+8\n\\end{aligned}\\)",
